239013671875, 0.03326416015625, 0.010223388671875, 0.0183868408203125, -0.0200653076171875, -0.03265380859375, 0.0016374588012695312, 0.00894927978515625, 0.01824951171875, -0.030242919921875, -0.0009613037109375, 0.029144287109375, -0.01378631591796875, -0.01407623291015625, -0.0219573974609375, -0.01287841796875, 0.01548004150390625, -0.00640869140625, -0.00785064697265625, -0.0158843994140625, -0.0171051025390625, 0.020050048828125, -0.02862548828125, 0.038360595703125, -0.019134521484375, 0.0137481689453125, -0.009674072265625, -0.039306640625, -0.0166015625, -0.0007328987121582031, 0.0076446533203125, -0.0172271728515625, -0.01197052001953125, 0.04315185546875, -0.006130218505859375, 0.01525115966796875, -0.01404571533203125, -0.00757598876953125, 0.034820556640625, 0.00847625732421875, 0.031585693359375, 0.0004582405090332031], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits. However, a key challenge remains: the ordinary differential equations that describe these systems must often be transformed from the real domain to the Laplace domain, and existing definitions of the Laplace transform do not readily extend to the framework of GA. In particular, prior work has addressed extensions of the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras, but a general and systematic definition within GA is still lacking.\n\nIn this work, we address this gap by introducing a definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra. Our approach builds on and generalizes previous results for hyper-complex algebras, providing a formulation that is specifically tailored to the structure of GA. We focus on geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5, where the signature refers to the number of positive and negative squares in the quadratic form that defines the algebra. This restriction is motivated by both mathematical tractability and practical relevance, as many physical systems of interest can be modeled within these signatures.\n\nThe main contribution of this paper is the proposed definition of the Laplace transform in GA and a discussion of its properties and applicability. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: we review relevant background, present our definition, and illustrate its use in the analysis of dynamical systems modeled within GA."}}